      "title": "Transforming First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "457b63fa-464a-509e-b9ba-a1fd3b1ff7ce",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+y=x^{3}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=1$ and $Q(x)=x^{3}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =e^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & =e^{\\int \\left(1\\right)\\,dx} \\\\  & =e^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)=e^{x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(e^{x}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}+y\\right) & =\\left(e^{x}\\right)\\cdot \\left(x^{3}\\right) \\\\ \\left(e^{x}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{x}\\right)\\cdot y & =\\left(e^{x}\\right)\\cdot \\left(x^{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(y\\cdot \\left(e^{x}\\right)\\right) & =\\left(e^{x}\\right)\\cdot \\left(x^{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "ecb16806-836b-5e8c-ba6f-fdfd157f8ae1",
